ole": "confounder_control"}, {"trace_id": "step_05", "role": "confounder_control"}, {"trace_id": "step_06", "role": "confounder_control"}, {"trace_id": "step_11", "role": "confounder_control"}, {"trace_id": "step_14", "role": "confounder_control"}, {"trace_id": "step_02", "role": "confounder_control"}, {"trace_id": "step_13", "role": "confounder_control"}], "narrative": "No abrupt regime switch. With the prescribed 5000-unit half-window, peak neighbourhood turnover is only 0.21 and the before/after contact sets overlap almost completely (step_01). The large edge-level diffs (jaccard 0.0-0.12, step_03/04/12) are misleading: they arise because different group members take turns being active over a fixed external contact set. Nodes 919, 1390 and 1860 each contact the identical ~30 external nodes in their respective active periods (step_07/08/10), while being wholly silent outside them (step_05/06); co-activity jaccards <=0.5 (step_14) and uniform per-member load (step_11) confirm a round-robin roster. The 0.61 turnover spikes at bandwidth 2500 (step_09) coincide with the near-empty bin [16551,18550] (step_02) and are sampling artifacts, collapsing to 0.03 on either side. Low external degrees (step_13) rule out popularity effects. This is stable structure with member rotation, not an old set of contacts giving way to a new one."}, "raw_final_response": "**Analysis**\n\nThe key finding: the group's *external contact set* is essentially constant across the entire period; what rotates is which group member is active.\n\n- **Group-level turnover is low.** At the prescribed half-window of 5000, peak turnover is only 0.21 (step_01), and the before/after contact lists at that peak are nearly identical (~31 of 32/36 shared). The series drifts slowly from 0.03 \u2192 0.21 \u2192 back to 0.16 \u2014 the signature of gradual background drift, not an abrupt old-set/new-set replacement.\n- **Edge-level \"churn\" is an artifact of member rotation.** step_03/step_04/step_12 report jaccard 0.0\u20130.12 with 60\u201390 edges added and removed, which looks dramatic. But inspecting the endpoints shows the *external* partners are the same list every time: 919 in [12551,17550] (step_08), 1390 in [17551,22550] (step_07), and 1860 in [25499,30499] (step_10) each contact the identical ~30 nodes {123,160,208,283,350,462,616,829,861,871,899,945,1057,...,1947,1950}. Only the group-side endpoint changes, so the edge tuples differ while the neighbourhood does not.\n- **Members take disjoint activity turns.** 919/1085 are active in [12551,17550] and silent in [25499,30499], while 1672/1860 show the reverse (step_05, step_06); co-activity jaccards are all \u22640.5 (step_14). Every member carries a similar ~91-event, ~31-partner load over the full window (step_11), i.e. a round-robin roster, not a regime change.\n- **Confounder controls.** Total volume is flat (~65\u201391 events per bin, step_02), so no burst drives the signal; the lone empty bin at [16551,18550] explains the spurious 0.61 spikes at bandwidth 2500 (step_09) \u2014 those are sampling artifacts from a quiet stretch, and turnover collapses back to 0.03 immediately on either side. The external contacts are low-degree (13\u201315 partners each, step_13), so this is not popularity-driven attachment.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"task_id\": \"t_8b4a3bfa\",\n  \"decision\": \"absent\",\n  \"mechanism\": \"regime_switch\",\n  \"entities\": [214, 315, 522, 860, 919, 1085, 1390, 1431, 1589, 1672, 1860, 1936],\n  \"window\": [2551, 32551],\n  \"confidence\": 0.83,\n  \"evidence\": [\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_01\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_09\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_07\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_08\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_10\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_03\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_04\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_12\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_05\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_06\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_11\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_14\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_02\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_13\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"}\n  ],\n  \"narrative\": \"No abrupt regime switch. With the prescribed 5000-unit half-window, peak neighbourhood turnover is only 0.21 and the before/after contact sets overlap almost completely (step_01). The large edge-level diffs (jaccard 0.0-0.12, step_03/04/12) are misleading: they arise because different group members take turns being active over a fixed external contact set. Nodes 919, 1390 and 1860 each contact the identical ~30 external nodes in their respective active periods (step_07/08/10), while being wholly silent outside them (step_05/06); co-activity jaccards <=0.5 (step_14) and uniform per-member load (step_11) confirm a round-robin roster. The 0.61 turnover spikes at bandwidth 2500 (step_09) coincide with the near-empty bin [16551,18550] (step_02) and are sampling artifacts, collapsing to 0.03 on either side. Low external degrees (step_13) rule out popularity effects. This is stable structure with member rotation, not an old set of contacts giving way to a new one.\"\n}\n```", "total_input_tokens": 63618, "total_output_tokens": 5768, "total_tokens": 69386, "total_latency_ms": 83173, "llm_calls": 7, "schema_repairs": 0, "status": "success", "error": ""}